//! `clipper2` — a pure-Rust, `std`-only re-implementation of the slice of the
//! Clipper2Lib C++ API that `tests/test1.cpp` exercises.
//!
//! The original translation unit is:
//!
//! ```cpp
//! #include "clipper2/clipper.h"
//! using namespace Clipper2Lib;
//!
//! Path64 path = {Point64(0,0), Point64(100,0), Point64(100,100), Point64(0,100)};
//! std::cout << path.size() << std::endl;
//! ```
//!
//! So the surface that has to exist, and behave exactly as the C++ one does, is:
//!
//! * [`Point64`] — an integer point with a two-argument constructor.
//! * [`Path64`] — an ordered, brace-initialisable sequence of points
//!   (`std::vector<Point64>` in C++) exposing `size()`.
//!
//! Everything reachable from those two types is modelled faithfully but
//! conservatively: this crate deliberately implements only behaviour that can be
//! derived from the public interface, rather than guessing at internals.
//!
//! Module layout:
//!
//! | module | contents |
//! |--------|----------|
//! | [`point`] | [`Point64`], [`PointD`] |
//! | [`path`]  | [`Path64`], [`PathD`], [`Paths64`], [`PathsD`] |
//! | [`rect`]  | [`Rect64`] and bounds computation |

// This file is compiled twice: as the `clipper2` library target, and again as a
// module of the `test1` binary (so that `rustc test1.rs` works with no Cargo).
// In the binary build the parts of the API the test does not call would each
// raise `dead_code`, and the re-exports below `unused_imports`. They are public
// library surface — exercised by the unit tests in each module — not dead code.
#![allow(dead_code, unused_imports)]

pub mod path;
pub mod point;
pub mod rect;

// `self::`-qualified so these re-exports resolve identically under every edition
// (a bare `use path::…` would be crate-root-relative in edition 2015, which is
// what plain `rustc test1.rs` selects when no `--edition` flag is passed).
pub use self::path::{Path64, PathD, Paths64, PathsD};
pub use self::point::{PointD, Point64};
pub use self::rect::Rect64;

